<<~ aka uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/operator-peer`>>



<<~ ahu slot=`#/entry`>>

! Async Flows ~ timing insight for human and agent

''Long ops must never go silent.'' Mining, writeback, multi-session sweeps, and
swarm-mines run seconds-to-minutes (a 9-spirit mine blew past 2 minutes blind).
The whole field has converged on one shape — ''submit → request-id → poll/wait'' —
and `lares` holds all three pieces: a ''daemon-job model'' (submit→id→poll),
''durable results by request-id'' (re-queryable after disconnect), and a ''dual''
''prose/JSON renderer''. The work reads as wiring, not invention (Beacon, cited).

> ''stdout is the API contract; progress and diagnostics ride stderr — and a''
''> timeout never loses work, because the result stays durable by id.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-contract`>>

!! The Contract ~ four verbs over the request-id

Every long op returns its ''request-id fast'' (the durable idempotency key we
already mint). On a TTY with no `--detach` it transparently attaches to `watch`;
off-TTY / `--detach` it returns the id and exits.

<<~ has entity=`Verb` name=`status` params=`lares vessel read <id> ~ one-shot, authoritative current state; emits a pollInterval hint`>>
<<~ has entity=`Verb` name=`watch` params=`lares watch <id> ~ stream progress until terminal; --exit-status mirrors the job result into the CLI exit code (gh/kubectl shape)`>>
<<~ has entity=`Verb` name=`wait` params=`lares wait <id> --timeout <s> ~ block until terminal-or-timeout; timeout → structured 'still running + progress', NEVER a hard fail (the work lives in the job, not the call)`>>
<<~ has entity=`Verb` name=`cancel` params=`lares cancel <id> ~ move the job to the cancelled terminal state (distinct from Ctrl-C, which DETACHES — job keeps running, re-attach by id)`>>

''As built'' (`packages/lares-cli/src/bin/lares.ts`): `lares receipt <requestId>` reads a request's
receipt from the vessel's own ledger and durable outcome doc, dispatching nothing; `lares cancel <requestId>`
marks the id abandoned only when no decision already stands, so a later request under that id refuses — it
stops no running job; and
`lares watch` names a source-directory ingest watcher. The four verbs above, the `pollInterval` hint,
exit 124 and the progress event stream read UNBUILT.

Precedent: `gh run watch --exit-status`, `kubectl wait --for=condition --timeout`,
`clig.dev` ("a start command that returns a job ID, and a status command to poll").

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-job-machine`>>

!! The Job Machine ~ five states, terminal-once

<<~ scale name=`job` ranks=`working ~ in progress -> input_required ~ awaiting the operator -> completed ~ ok, terminal -> failed ~ error, terminal -> cancelled ~ stopped, terminal`>>

''Terminal-once:'' once a job reaches completed/failed/cancelled it never moves
again. ''Poll carries the authority'' (not pushed notifications, which drop) — non-terminal
= keep working, terminal = stop + fetch the result. `status` emits a server-side
''`pollInterval`'' (ms) so an agent polls without overloading (the MCP async-task
shape). A caller fetches the result after terminal, by id.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/dual-surface`>>

!! The Dual Surface ~ one event stream, two projections

The load-bearing split (every source agrees): ''stdout = the machine-readable''
''result (the API contract); progress + diagnostics → stderr.'' Switch on `isatty`.
One progress event stream; the existing renderer splits it:

* ''TTY (human):'' a redrawing bar / spinner / elapsed-heartbeat on ''stderr''
  (`\r` rewrite, animation only when isatty) — stdout stays clean.
* ''off-TTY / `--json` (agent):'' ''NDJSON progress lines'' (Terraform `apply_progress`
  + BuildKit `rawjson` shape), result alone on stdout:

```text
{"type":"progress","requestId":"…","phase":"mine","done":3,"total":9,"elapsedSeconds":47,"etaSeconds":94}
{"type":"progress","requestId":"…","phase":"writeback","done":9,"total":9,"elapsedSeconds":118}
{"type":"result","requestId":"…","status":"completed","ok":true, …}      # stdout
```

`render.ts`'s prose/JSON shore carries the RESULT half of this split (prose on a TTY or under `--no-json`, one structured
payload on stdout off-TTY or under `--json`); the progress stream reads UNBUILT.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/progress-eta`>>

!! Progress & ETA

* ''Never silent:'' show something in <100ms; never a blank wait (clig.dev).
* ''Determinate'' (count/total → % · bar · ETA) when the job knows its total — the swarm
  mine knows "9 spirits," a sweep knows its file count. ''Indeterminate'' (spinner /
  elapsed-seconds heartbeat) when it knows none.
* ''ETA'' by an ''exponential moving average of per-item rate'' (tqdm) — recent items
  weighted, so ETA tracks rate changes without jitter.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/timeouts-cancel-resume`>>

!! Timeouts · Cancellation · Resumability

* ''Heartbeat, not hard-fail.'' The 2-minute silent overrun cure: the work lives in
  the daemon-job (unbounded, heartbeating); the foreground `wait --timeout` returns
  a ''structured "still running, here's progress"'' (with the requestId + current
  `{phase,done,total,eta}`), never killing the work.
* ''Exit codes:'' ''124 = timeout'' (GNU `timeout` convention, so scripts/agents tell
  timeout from failure) + semantic codes (0 ok · 1 general · 2 usage · 3 not-found ·
  4 denied · 5 conflict).
* ''Cancellation:'' Ctrl-C ''detaches'' (job runs on, re-attach by id); `cancel <id>`
  ''stops'' (cancelled terminal state). On SIGINT: say so immediately, then time-boxed
  cleanup.
* ''No-loss by construction:'' the ''durable request-id SERVES AS the idempotency key''
  (Stripe/MCP shape) — a re-submit returns the stored result if completed, the
  in-progress handle if running; a timeout stays re-queryable, never lost.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/agent-ergonomics`>>

!! Agent Ergonomics ~ never block the agent's context

* ''fire-and-get-id'', poll-by-id — an orchestrating agent submits, gets the id,
  and polls; it never holds a blocking call across its context.
* `wait --timeout` returns ''"PROCESSING: job <id> still running (original ask: …)"''
  — carrying the context so the agent needn't hold it.
* ''`--yes` / `--force`'' (an agent cannot type `y`; a prompt-hang kills its workflow).
* ''Parseable error CODES, not prose'' (`not-found`, not "Error occurred").

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/map-and-backlog`>>

!! Map to Our Infra + Backlog

Three pieces stand:

<<~ scale name=`have` ranks=```daemon-jobs ~ mempalace `--daemon --background` → job-id → daemon jobs/wait/status (we call mine SYNCHRONOUSLY instead) -> durable-results ~ lares summons → daemon-plane/outcomes/<requestId> (CRDT, survives a CLI timeout, re-queryable) -> dual-render ~ render.ts prose/JSON (the progress-event split shore)```>>

''Implementation order'' (write-toward; confirm interfaces first — see caveat):
# Make long ops ''return the request-id fast'' (+ `--detach`); default TTY attaches to `watch`.
# Add ''`lares vessel read|watch|wait|cancel <id>`'' over the durable-result + daemon-job stores.
# Emit the ''progress event stream'' (job → NDJSON on stderr / bar on TTY; result on stdout); route mempalace `--daemon --background` for the heavy mines + surface its `daemon jobs/wait`.
# ''Exit-124 timeout'' + the semantic codes + `pollInterval` in `status`.

''Caveat (Beacon, Cup):'' the research did not read the repo — confirm whether the
daemon-job/durable-result interfaces already emit //intermediate// progress (vs only
terminal status) before binding the verbs. The event shape above names the target to
write toward; it describes no current emission.

<<~/ahu>>

